# SUVL: Suave Values

SUVL is Suave's own data format. It is meant for config files and data you edit by hand: friendly like YAML, exact like JSON, and typed like TOML. Files end in `.suvl`.

```suvl
# a SUVL file
name: "Ada Lovelace"
tags: [math, poetry, 3.5]          # bare words are text; numbers, true, false, none are typed

server {                           # a block: key { ... } means key: { ... }
  host: localhost
  port: 8080
  timeout: 90s                     # durations become seconds
  cache: 64mb                      # sizes become bytes
  ratio: 50%                       # percentages become fractions (0.5)
}

born: 1815-12-10                   # dates and date-times become dates
accent: #ff8800                    # hex colors stay text
point: {x: 1, y: 2}

bio: """
  Several lines,
  dedented to the least indented one.
"""
```

## Rules

**Entries.** `key: value` (or `key = value`) or a block `key { ... }`. Entries end at a new line or a comma, so `a: 1, b: 2` on one line is fine. A key is a bare word (`name`, `max-size`, `a.b`) or a quoted string. A key may appear only once in a map.

**Comments.** `#` and `//` run to the end of the line. A `#` followed by 3, 4, 6 or 8 hex digits is a color, not a comment.

**Values.**

| You write | You get |
| --- | --- |
| `"text"` with escapes `\n \t \" \\ \u{1F600}` | text |
| `'text'` | text, no escapes |
| `"""..."""` | multi-line text, dedented |
| `hello` `/usr/bin` `a-b` | text (a bare word runs to a space, comma, bracket or quote) |
| `42` `-3.5` `1e3` `1_000` `0xFF` `0b101` `0o17` | numbers (huge whole numbers become big integers) |
| `inf` `-inf` `nan` | special numbers |
| `true` `false` | booleans |
| `none` (or `null`) | none |
| `[a, b, c]` | list (commas or new lines between items; a trailing comma is fine) |
| `{a: 1, b: 2}` | map |
| `2026-11-17` `2026-11-17T10:30:00Z` | a date |
| `90s` `5m` `2h` `1d` `1w` `250ms` | seconds as a number (`250ms` is `0.25`) |
| `512b` `4kb` `10mb` `1gb` `2tb` | bytes as a number (1024 based) |
| `50%` | a fraction (`0.5`) |
| `#ff8800` | text `"#ff8800"` |

Use `suvl.parse_raw(text)` to keep durations, sizes and percentages as text.

**The top level** is normally a map. A whole file can also be a single list (`[1, 2, 3]`), a brace map, or a single value (`42`). An empty file is an empty map.

## Writing it back

`suvl.stringify(value)` gives a canonical file: one entry per line, nested maps as blocks, short lists on one line, text bare when that is safe and quoted otherwise, dates as `YYYY-MM-DD` (or full date-times). Reading what you wrote gives the same value.

## Using it from Suave

```suave
use std.suvl
let cfg = suvl.parse(fs.read("app.suvl"))
print(cfg.server.timeout)                  // 90
fs.write("out.suvl", suvl.stringify({name: "x", tags: ["a", "b"]}))
```

`std.data` can change a file between JSON, YAML, TOML, SUVL and CSV: `suave convert app.yaml suvl`.

## Why a new format?

* **Durations and sizes are first-class.** `timeout: 90s` reads better than `timeout_seconds: 90`, and a wrong unit is a parse error.
* **Bare words need no quotes**, but everything is still unambiguous: only the listed words (`true`, `false`, `none`, numbers, dates, units) change type, and the writer quotes any text that would be misread.
* **Errors say where**: line, column and the source line with a caret.
* It is small enough to hold in your head: one page.
